---
import AdSlot from '../components/AdSlot.astro';
import CodonChart from '../components/CodonChart.astro';
import Faq from '../components/Faq.astro';
import WorkedExamples from '../components/WorkedExamples.astro';
import { faqs } from '../lib/faqs.mjs';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import DnaTranslator from '../components/DnaTranslator.astro';
import { AMINO_ACIDS, CODON_TABLE, STOP_CODONS, codingStrandFrom, transcribe, codons, anticodons, translate, proteinSummary, findStartCodon } from '../lib/molecular.mjs';
const codonCount = abbr => Object.values(CODON_TABLE).filter(a => a.abbr === abbr).length;
const sixfold = AMINO_ACIDS.filter(a => codonCount(a.abbr) === 6).map(a => a.name.toLowerCase());
const single = AMINO_ACIDS.filter(a => codonCount(a.abbr) === 1).map(a => a.name.toLowerCase());
const senseCodons = 64 - STOP_CODONS.length;
// Worked examples are computed with the translator's own functions, so the answers always match the tool.
const spaced = seq => seq.match(/.{1,3}/g).join(' ');
const mrnaOf = (seq, kind) => transcribe(codingStrandFrom(seq, kind));
const protein = rna => { const at = findStartCodon(rna); return proteinSummary(translate(rna, { frame: Math.max(0, at) % 3, fromStartCodon: at >= 0 })).abbrs; };
const agt = mrnaOf('AGT', 'template');
const strand12 = mrnaOf('TACGGTAGTACC', 'template');
const given = 'AACUAUGUGGCAU';
const flow = mrnaOf('TACGGACTT', 'template');
const alignment = [
  ['Coding strand (DNA)', '5′', 'ATG GCC TGA', '3′'],
  ['Template strand (DNA)', '3′', 'TAC CGG ACT', '5′'],
  ['mRNA', '5′', 'AUG GCC UGA', '3′'],
  ['tRNA anticodons', '3′', 'UAC CGG ···', '5′'],
  ['Protein', '', 'Met – Ala, released at the UGA stop', ''],
];
const examples = [
  { question: 'A triplet of bases in the template strand of DNA is AGT. What would be the corresponding codon for mRNA?', steps: ['The mRNA is complementary to the template strand: A pairs with U, G with C, and T with A.', `AGT → ${agt}. RNA has no thymine, so TCA cannot be right.`], answer: `${agt}, which codes for ${CODON_TABLE[agt].name.toLowerCase()} (${CODON_TABLE[agt].abbr}).` },
  { question: 'How many codons will result from the transcription of this DNA strand: T A C G G T A G T A C C?', steps: [`Transcribe the template: TACGGTAGTACC → mRNA ${spaced(strand12)}.`, `12 bases ÷ 3 bases per codon = ${codons(strand12).length} codons.`], answer: `${codons(strand12).length} codons, translated as ${protein(strand12)}.` },
  { question: 'An mRNA has the sequence 5′-AACUAUGUGGCAU-3′. What is the translated protein sequence from N-terminus to C-terminus?', steps: [`Ribosomes start at the first AUG, which begins at base ${findStartCodon(given) + 1}: AACU | AUG UGG CAU.`, 'Look up each codon: AUG = Met, UGG = Trp, CAU = His.'], answer: `${protein(given)} (N-terminus → C-terminus).` },
  { question: 'Write the mRNA, the tRNA anticodons and the amino acids for the DNA template 3′-TAC GGA CTT-5′.', steps: [`mRNA (complement, U for T): 5′-${spaced(flow)}-3′.`, `Anticodons pair with each codon: 3′-${spaced(anticodons(flow))}-5′.`], answer: `${protein(flow)}.` },
];
---
<Layout title="DNA to mRNA Converter: Transcribe & Translate DNA to Protein" section="DNA translator" breadcrumbs={[['Tools', '/tools/'], ['DNA to mRNA converter', '/dna-transcription-translation/']]} description="Convert DNA to mRNA and protein: paste a coding strand, template strand, or mRNA to get the mRNA, tRNA anticodons, codons, and amino acid chain, with every step.">
<div class="intro"><div><div class="eyebrow">Molecular biology, base by base</div><h1>DNA to mRNA converter and protein translator</h1><p>Paste a DNA coding strand, a template strand, or an mRNA sequence. We transcribe it into mRNA, line up the tRNA anticodons, read the codons in your chosen frame, and build the amino acid chain, with every step shown and every base color-coded.</p></div></div>
<nav class="related page-jump" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#how-to-transcribe">How to convert DNA to mRNA ↓</a><a href="#worked-examples">Worked examples ↓</a><a href="#codon-chart-heading">Codon chart ↓</a><a href="/codon-chart/">Codon lookup &amp; amino acid table ↗</a></nav>
<DnaTranslator/>
<AdSlot type="rectangle" />
<section class="explanation" id="how-to-transcribe" aria-labelledby="how-to-transcribe-heading">
  <h2 id="how-to-transcribe-heading">How to convert DNA to mRNA, step by step</h2>
  <p>A gene has two DNA strands. The <strong>template strand</strong> (antisense) is the one RNA polymerase reads, 3′ → 5′. The <strong>coding strand</strong> (sense) is its partner, and it has the same sequence as the mRNA except that DNA uses T where RNA uses U. Which rule you apply depends on which strand the question gives you.</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>From the template strand:</strong> write the complementary base for each one, using U instead of T. A → U, T → A, G → C, C → G. So 3′-TAC-5′ becomes 5′-AUG-3′, the start codon.</li>
    <li><strong>From the coding strand:</strong> copy it and swap every T for U. 5′-ATG GCC-3′ becomes 5′-AUG GCC-3′.</li>
    <li><strong>Find the reading frame:</strong> ribosomes start at the first AUG and read three bases at a time. Split the mRNA into codons from there.</li>
    <li><strong>Translate:</strong> look each codon up in the codon chart below until you reach a stop codon (UAA, UAG, or UGA).</li>
  </ol>
  <div class="fact-table-wrap"><table class="fact-table dna-align"><caption class="codon-caption">One short gene, every layer lined up. Each tRNA anticodon pairs with the codon directly above it; stop codons have no tRNA.</caption><tbody>{alignment.map(([label, left, seq, right]) => <tr><th scope="row">{label}</th><td><code>{left ? `${left}-${seq}-${right}` : seq}</code></td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
  <h3>From mRNA to tRNA anticodons</h3>
  <p>Each tRNA carries an anticodon that base-pairs with one codon, antiparallel, so it is written 3′ → 5′ under the codon. Take the complement of each codon base: the codon 5′-AUG-3′ is read by the anticodon 3′-UAC-5′, which carries methionine. The translator shows this as the bottom row of the strand display. Because of third-position “wobble” pairing, one tRNA can often read more than one codon.</p>
  <h3>From mRNA or protein back to DNA</h3>
  <p>To go from mRNA to DNA, reverse the logic: swap U for T to get the coding strand, then take the complement to get the template strand. Choose “mRNA” in the translator and it does this for you. Going from a protein back to DNA has no single answer, because most amino acids have several codons: leucine alone has six.</p>
</section>
<WorkedExamples items={examples} title="DNA to mRNA practice problems, solved" intro="Exam-style questions on transcription and translation, with the working. Paste any of these sequences into the converter above and pick the matching strand type to check."/>
<section class="explanation" aria-labelledby="codon-chart-heading">
  <h2 id="codon-chart-heading">Genetic code chart</h2>
  <p>The genetic code is the dictionary a ribosome uses to turn mRNA into protein. Each three-base codon names one amino acid or a stop signal. Find the codon’s first base in the left column, its second base in the next column, and its third base along the top: the cell where they meet is the amino acid. AUG both encodes methionine and marks where translation begins, and the three highlighted stop codons (UAA, UAG, UGA) end it. For a searchable version and an amino-acid-to-codon table, open the <a class="text-link" href="/codon-chart/">codon chart</a>.</p>
  <CodonChart/>
  <p>Because 64 codons cover only 20 amino acids, the code is <strong>degenerate</strong>: {senseCodons} sense codons share the work, and most amino acids have more than one. {sixfold.map(n => n[0].toUpperCase() + n.slice(1)).join(', ')} each have six codons, while {single.join(' and ')} have just one. Notice that synonymous codons usually differ only in the third base, which is why a change there is often a silent mutation and why one tRNA can read several codons through third-position “wobble” pairing.</p>
  <p>The chart is written in mRNA letters. To use it with a gene, take the coding strand of the DNA and swap every T for U, or read the template strand and write its complement. If a stop codon appears early in a reading frame, the frame is probably not the one the cell uses: try the other two frames, or tick “Begin at first AUG” in the translator above.</p>
</section>
<div class="related"><a href="/codon-chart/">Codon chart &amp; amino acid lookup ↗</a><a href="/learn/transcription-and-translation/">Transcription &amp; translation explained ↗</a><a href="/learn/dna-structure/">DNA structure &amp; base pairing ↗</a><a href="/cells/ribosome/">The ribosome ↗</a><a href="/practice/">Practice questions ↗</a></div>
<AdSlot type="native" />
<Faq items={faqs['dna-transcription-translation']} title="DNA to mRNA questions"/>
</Layout>
